{"groups":[{"members":["c1","c2"],"primary":"c1","title":"New Jersey Security Deposit Law: deposit cap, interest-bearing account, notice and 30-day return","requirement":"If a landlord collects a security deposit, it may not exceed 1.5 times one month's rent, with any yearly increase capped at 10% of the current deposit. The deposit must be held in trust in an insured New Jersey interest-bearing account (interest belongs to the tenant), with written notice of the account details given to the tenant, and must be returned with interest, less itemized deductions, within 30 days after the tenancy ends.","key_value":"Max deposit 1.5x one month's rent; yearly increase max 10% of current deposit; max 2.5x monthly rent at lease inception (deposit plus first month's rent); return within 30 days of tenancy termination (5 business days if tenant is displaced by fire, flood, condemnation or evacuation; 15 business days after a Safe Housing Act termination); double damages for wrongful withholding","status":"in_force","effective_date":null,"end_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-19 to 46:8-21.5","coverage_conditions":"Most residential rental properties, including mobile homes. A tenant in an owner-occupied two- or three-family dwelling may make the law apply 30 days after sending the landlord a written request.","exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings are exempt unless the tenant sends a written request, which takes effect after 30 days. The interest-bearing account provisions do not apply to seasonal rentals, meaning a term of 125 consecutive days or less by a person with a permanent residence elsewhere. The landlord bears the burden of proving seasonal use.","coverage_from":"c1","conflict_note":null}],"dropped":[]}
